        "questions": [
            {
                "type": "lcm-prime-match",
                "prompt": "12 = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 3, 18 = 2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3. Jo says the LCM is 12 \u00d7 18 = 216. Is she right?",
                "options": [
                    "Yes. 216 is a multiple of both.",
                    "No. The LCM is 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 3 = 36.",
                    "No. The LCM is 2 \u00d7 3 = 6.",
                    "No. The LCM is 18, the bigger number."
                ],
                "feedback": [
                    "216 is a common multiple, but not the lowest. 12 \u00d7 18 uses the shared 2 and 3 twice.",
                    "The overlap 2, 3 once, plus 2 from 12 and 3 from 18.",
                    "That's the HCF: the overlap only.",
                    "18 isn't a multiple of 12."
                ],
                "answer": 1
            },
            {
                "type": "lcm-prime-match",
                "prompt": "20 = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 5, 30 = 2 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 5. Ben multiplies the overlap and gets 10. Has he found the LCM?",
                "options": [
                    "Yes. 10 is the LCM.",
                    "No. Multiply every tile: 2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 3 = 60.",
                    "No. The LCM is 20 \u00d7 30 = 600.",
                    "No. The LCM is 2 \u00d7 5 \u00d7 3 = 30."
                ],
                "feedback": [
                    "10 is the HCF. The LCM must be a multiple of both numbers, so it can't be smaller than either.",
                    "Right. Multiply the overlap once and both sides.",
                    "600 is a common multiple, but not the lowest. It uses the shared 2 and 5 twice.",
                    "That leaves out 20's extra 2. 30 isn't a multiple of 20."
                ],
                "answer": 1
            },
            {
                "type": "lcm-prime-match",
                "prompt": "8 = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2, 12 = 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 3. Priya multiplies the overlap and 12's side: 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 3 = 12. What is the LCM?",
                "options": [
                    "12",
                    "24",
                    "96",
                    "4"
                ],
                "feedback": [
                    "12 isn't a multiple of 8. Include the 2 on 8's side.",
                    "2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 3 = 24. 24 \u00f7 8 = 3 and 24 \u00f7 12 = 2.",
                    "That's 8 \u00d7 12. It uses the shared 2, 2 twice.",
                    "That's the HCF: the overlap only."
                ],
                "answer": 1
            },
            {
                "type": "lcm-prime-match",
                "prompt": "9 = 3 \u00d7 3, 10 = 2 \u00d7 5. Nothing is in the overlap. What is the LCM?",
                "options": [
                    "1",
                    "19",
                    "45",
                    "90"
                ],
                "feedback": [
                    "1 is the HCF. The LCM must be a multiple of both numbers.",
                    "You added 9 + 10. Multiply every tile.",
                    "That leaves out the 2. 45 isn't a multiple of 10.",
                    "Nothing shared, so multiply every tile: 3 \u00d7 3 \u00d7 2 \u00d7 5 = 90."
                ],
                "answer": 3
            }
